¶
{{smaller block|This joy, when God speaks peace to the soul, is ineffabile gaudium. . . . It gives end to all jars, doubts, and differences, . . . and makes a man ''keep Hilary-term'' all his life.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 68.}}}}

'''To make terms''', to come to an agreement.—'''To speak in term†''', to speak in precise language, or in set terms. See def. 11.

{{smaller block|Seyde I nat wel? I can not ''speke in terme.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Pardoner's Tale, l. 25.}}}}

'''To stand upon one's terms†''', to insist upon conditions: followed by ''with.''

{{smaller block|I had rather be the most easy, tame, and resigned believer in the most gross and imposing church in the world . . . than one of those great and philosophical minds who ''stand upon their terms with'' God.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', Sermons, II. viii.}}}}

'''Total term.''' See ''partial term'', above.—'''Transcendent term''', a term which signifies something not included under any of the ten predicaments, especially ''everything'' and ''nothing.''—'''Trinity term.''' See def. 6 (''a'') and (''b'').—'''Vague term''', a word or phrase sometimes used as a term, but without fixed meaning. ='''Syn. 11.''' ''Word, Term, Expression, Phrase'', vocable, name. ''Word'' is generic; ''term'' and ''expression'' are specific: every ''term'' is a ''word;'' a ''phrase'' is a combination of ''words'' generally less than a sentence; an ''expression'' is generally either a ''word'' or a ''phrase'', but may be a sentence. A ''term'' is, in this connection, especially a ''word'' of exact meaning: as, "phlebitis" is a medical ''term.'' See ''diction.''

'''term''' (tėrm), ''v. t.'' [Early mod. E. also ''tearm;'' < ''term'', ''n.''] To name; call; denominate; designate.

{{smaller block|A certeine pamphlet which he ''termed'' a cooling carde for Philautus, yet generally to be applyed to all louers.
{{right|''Lyly'', Euphues, Anat. of Wit, p. 105.}}
Britan hath bin anciently ''term'd'' Albion, both by the Greeks and Romans.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., i.}}}}

