readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|I'le march where my Captaine leads, wer't into the Presence of the great ''Termagaunt.''
{{right|''Heywood'', Royal King (Works, ed. Pearson, 1874, VI. 23).}}}}

2†. A turbulent, brawling person, male or female.

{{smaller block|This terrible ''termagant'', this Nero, this Pharaoh.
{{right|''Bp. Bale'', Yet a Course at the Romyshe Foxe, fol. 39 b [(1543). (''Latham.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|Wealth may do us good service, but if it get the mastery of our trust it will turn tyrant, ''termagant;'' we condemn ourselves to our own galleys.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 149.}}}}

3. A boisterous, brawling, or turbulent woman; a shrew; a virago; a scold.

{{smaller block|She threw his periwig into the fire. Well, said he, thou art a brave ''termagant.'' {{right|''Tatler.''}}}}

{{smaller block|If she [woman] be passionate, want of manners makes her a ''termagant'' and a scold, which is much at one with Lunatic. {{right|''Defoe'' (Arber's Eng. Garner, II. 267).}}}}

II. ''a.'' Violent; turbulent; boisterous; quarrelsome; scolding; of women, shrewish.

{{smaller block|'Twas time to counterfeit, or that hot ''termagant'' Scot had paid me scot and lot too. {{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., v. 4. 114.}}}}

{{smaller block|Yet it is oftentimes too late with some of you young, ''termagant'', flashy sinners — you have all the guilt of the intention, and none of the pleasure of the practice.
{{right|''Congreve'', Old Bachelor, i. 4.}}
Hath any man a ''termagant'' wife?
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 136.}}}}

¶
'''termagantly''' (tėr′ma-gant-li), ''adv.'' In a termagant, boisterous, or scolding manner; like a termagant; outrageously; scandalously. ''Tom Brown'', Works, II. 148. (''Davies.'')
